==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{proto|Germanic|tīra-|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|dei-|lang=ang}}.

Cognate with Old Norse {{term|tírr||glory, renown|lang=non}}. A variant of {{proto|Germanic|tērīn-|lang=ang}}, gave Old High German {{term|zieri|zierī|lang=goh}} (German {{term|Zier||splendour, beauty|lang=de}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/tiːr/|lang=ang}}, {{X-SAMPA|/ti:r/}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|head=tīr|g=m}}

# [[fame]], [[glory]]

----

==Old Welsh==

===Etymology===
From {{proto|Celtic|tīros|lang=owl}} from {{proto|Indo-European|ters-|dry|lang=owl}}, i.e. "dry land" as opposed to lake or sea. Cognates include English {{term|thirst|lang=en}}, Latin {{term|terra|lang=la}}.

===Noun===
{{head|owl|noun|g=m}}

# [[land]]

====Descendants====
* Welsh: {{l|cy|tir}}

